== 2. The Dangers of Duty

#strong[The second #emph[device] that Satan has to draw souls from holy duties, and to keep them away from religious services, is,]

By presenting to them the danger, the losses, and the sufferings that attend the performance of such and such religious services. By this device Satan kept those who believed in Christ quiet, and kept them from confessing Christ, in John 12:42: “Nevertheless, many of the leaders believed in Him; but because of the Pharisees they did not confess Him, for fear that they would be put out of the synagogue.” “I would walk in all the ways of God; I would give myself up to the strictest ways of holiness; but I am afraid that dangers will attend me on the one hand, and losses, and perhaps such and such sufferings, on the other,” says many a man. Oh, how should we help ourselves against this temptation and device of Satan?

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider that all the troubles and afflictions that you#super[+] meet with in a way of righteousness will never hurt you; they will never harm you. “Who can harm#footnote[#emph[Nemo proprie laeditur nisi a seipso.] Nobody is properly hurt except by himself and his own fault.] you#super[+] if you are zealous for what is good?” says the apostle; that is, no one will harm you#super[+]. Natural conscience cannot help paying homage to the image of God stamped on the natures, words, works, and lives of the godly, as we may see in the conduct of Nebuchadnezzar and#footnote[Gordius, that blessed martyr, counted it a loss to him not to suffer many kinds of tortures. He says tortures are only tradings with God for glory. The greater the combat, the greater the reward that follows.] Darius toward Daniel. All the afflictions and troubles that attend men in a way of righteousness can never rob them of their treasure, of their jewels. They may rob them of some light, slight things, such as the sword that is by their side, or the stick that is in their hand, or the flowers or ribbons that are in their hats. The treasure of a saint is the presence of God, the favor of God, union and communion with God, the pardon of sin, the joy of the Spirit, the peace of conscience; and these are jewels that no one can give but Christ, and no one can take away but Christ. Now, why should a gracious soul keep away from a way of holiness because of afflictions, etc., when no afflictions can strip a man of his heavenly jewels, which are his ornaments and his safety here, and will be his happiness and glory hereafter? Why should that man be afraid or troubled about#footnote[That was a notable saying of Luther: “Let him who died for my soul see to its salvation.”] storms at sea whose treasures are secure in a friend’s hand on land? Why, a believer’s treasure is always safe in the hands of Christ: his life is safe, his soul is safe, his grace is safe, his comfort is safe, and his crown is safe in the hand of Christ. “I know whom#footnote[2 Tim 1:12.] I have believed, and I am convinced that He is able to guard what I have entrusted to Him for that day,” says the apostle. The child’s most precious things are most secure in his father’s hands; so are our souls, our graces, and our comforts in the hand of Christ.

The second #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider that other precious saints, who were shining#footnote[William Flower, martyr, said, “Heaven shall as soon fall as I will forsake my profession, or budge in the least degree from it.” So Sanctus, under as great torment as you have read of, cried out (#emph[Christianus sum]), “I am a Christian”; no torment could bring him to turn from the service of his God. I might produce a cloud of witnesses; but if these do not move you to be noble and brave, I am afraid more will not.] lights on earth and are now triumphing in heaven, have held on in religious services, despite all the troubles and dangers that surrounded them. Nehemiah and Ezra were surrounded with dangers on the left hand and on the right, and yet in the face of them all they held on building the temple and the wall of Jerusalem. So did Daniel, and those precious worthies in Ps 44: under a lack of outward encouragements, and in the face of a world of very great discouragements, their souls clung to God and his ways. Though they were crushed in the lair of jackals and covered with the shadow of death; indeed, though they were counted all day long as sheep to be slaughtered, yet their hearts did not turn back, nor did their steps stray from his ways. Though bonds and imprisonment awaited Paul and the rest of the apostles in every place, yet they held on in the work and service of the Lord. Why then should you#super[+] fall away from their worthy examples, which it is your duty and your glory to follow?

The third #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that all the troubles and dangers that attend the performance of holy duties and heavenly services are only temporal and momentary, but the neglect of them may lay you#super[+] open to all temporal, spiritual, and eternal dangers. “How shall we escape if we neglect such a great salvation?” He does not say, if we reject or renounce such a great salvation, but if we neglect, or shift off (as the word is), “such#footnote[ἀμελήσαντες. Disregard; not care for it.] a great salvation.” He does not say, how shall we escape if we oppose or persecute such a great salvation? No, but if we let slip or neglect such a great salvation, how shall we escape? That is, we cannot escape by any way, or means, or device in the world; divine justice will be above us, in spite of our very souls. Doing such and such heavenly services may lay you#super[+] open to the frowns of men, but neglecting them will lay you open to the frowns of God. Doing them may make you#super[+] contemptible in the eyes of men,#footnote[Francis Xavier counseled John the Third, king of Portugal, to meditate every day for a quarter of an hour on that text, “What will it profit a man if he gains the whole world, yet forfeits his soul?”] but neglecting them may make you contemptible in the eyes of God. Doing them may be the loss of your estate, but neglecting them may be the loss of God, Christ, heaven, and your soul forever. Doing them may shut you out from some outward, temporal contentments; neglecting them may shut you out from that excellent, matchless glory which “no eye has seen, no ear has heard, no heart has imagined.” Remember this: there is no man who breathes who will not suffer more by neglecting those holy and heavenly services that God commands, commends, and rewards than he can possibly suffer by doing them, etc.

The fourth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider that God knows how to deliver from troubles by troubles, from afflictions by afflictions, from dangers by dangers. By lesser troubles and afflictions God often delivers his people from greater ones, so that they will say, “We would have perished if we had not perished; we would have been undone if we had not been#footnote[Periissem nisi periissem.] undone; we would have been in danger if we had not been in danger.” God will so order all the afflictions that befall you#super[+] in the way of righteousness that your#footnote[#emph[Non essem ego salvus nisi istae periissent. Anaxagoras:] had these things not perished, I could not have been safe, said this philosopher when he saw great possessions that he had lost.] souls will say, “We would not for all the world have missed meeting with such and such troubles and afflictions; for surely, if these had not befallen us, it would have gone worse and worse with us.” Oh, the carnal security, pride, formality, dead-heartedness, lukewarmness, censoriousness, and earthliness that God has cured us of by the troubles and dangers that we have met with in the ways and services of the Lord! I remember a story of a godly man who, as he was going to take ship for France, broke his leg; and it pleased providence so to order it that the ship he should have gone in was cast away at that very time, and not a man saved. So by breaking a bone his life was saved. So the Lord many times breaks our bones, but it is in order to save our lives and our souls forever. He gives us a potion that makes us heart-sick, but it is in order to make us perfectly well, and to purge us of those bad humors that have made our heads ache, and God’s heart ache, and our souls sick and heavy to the death, etc. Oh, therefore, do not let any danger or misery hinder you from your duty.

The fifth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is solemnly to consider that you#super[+] will gain more in the service of God, and by walking in righteous and holy ways, though troubles and afflictions should attend you, than you can possibly suffer or lose by being found in the service of God. “Godliness … is great gain.” Oh, the joy, the peace,#footnote[Tertullian, in his book to the martyrs, has an apt saying (#emph[Negotiatio est aliquid amittere ut majora lucreris]), that is, it is right and good trading when something is parted with to gain more. He applies it to their sufferings, in which, though the flesh lost something, yet the spirit gained much more.] the comfort, the rest that saints meet with in the ways and service of God! They find that religious services are not empty things, but things in which God is pleased to reveal his beauty and glory to their souls. “My soul thirsts for God” (says David), “that I might see Your beauty and Your glory, as I have seen You in the sanctuary.” Oh, the sweet looks, the sweet words, the sweet hints, the sweet nudges, the sweet influences, the sweet love letters that gracious souls have from heaven when they wait on God in holy and heavenly services! The least of these will eclipse and outweigh all the splendor and glory of this world, and richly repay the soul for all the troubles, afflictions, and dangers that have attended it in the service of God. Oh, the saints can say under all their troubles and afflictions that they have food to eat and drink to#footnote[When the noble general Zelislaus had lost his hand in the wars of the king of Poland, the king sent him a golden hand in its place. What we lose in Christ’s service, he will make up by giving us some golden mercies.] drink that the world does not know of; that they have such inflowings, such refreshments, such warmings, etc., as they would not exchange for all the honors, riches, and delicacies of this world. Ah! let a Christian only compare his external losses with his spiritual, internal, and eternal gain, and he will find that for every penny he loses in the service of God he gains a pound; for every pound he loses he gains a hundred; for every hundred lost he gains a thousand. We lose pins in his service and find pearls. We lose the favor of the creature, and peace with the creature, and perhaps the comforts and contentments of the creature; and we gain the favor of God, peace with conscience, and the comforts and contentments of a better life. Ah! if the men of this world knew the sweetness that saints enjoy in afflictions, they would rather choose Manasseh’s iron chain than his golden crown; they would rather be Paul a prisoner than Paul caught up to the third heaven. For light afflictions they will have a#footnote[Though the cross is bitter, yet it is only short: a little storm, as one said of Julian’s persecution, and an eternal calm follows.] weight of glory; for a few afflictions they will have those joys, pleasures, and contentments that are like the stars of heaven or the sands of the sea, which cannot be numbered; for momentary afflictions they will have an eternal crown of glory. “It is only shutting your eyes, and you will be in heaven at once,” said the martyr. Oh, therefore, do not let any afflictions or troubles bring you to shun the ways of God, or to quit that service which should be dearer to you than a world, indeed, than your very life, etc.
